INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Conti investigation source batch", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002; SRC-003; SRC-004", "text": "SRC-001 (Rewards for Justice) states Conti, also known as Wizard Spider, is assessed by the U.S. government as a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S./Western critical infrastructure. It says first detected 2019, more than 1,000 ransomware operations, more than 400 organizations worldwide victimized and over 290 in U.S.; typical method is stealing and encrypting files, demanding ransom via portal, and selling/publishing stolen data if unpaid. It lists online aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis as believed linked to Conti, associated location Russia, and associated applications Conti ransomware-as-a-service, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. It says Conti pledged Russian support after the February 2022 invasion and threatened infrastructure of countries perceived hostile to Russia. SRC-002 (Rapid7) reports a Feb. 27, 2022 ContiLeaks disclosure of 14 MB of chats across 393 JSON files, some encrypted and incomplete, including internal communications, tool screenshots, exploit/design discussions, credentials and alleged server access details; it says the leak scale suggested a senior member or disgruntled affiliates. The chats showed interviewing operators, coding exercises, salary negotiations, products, a large Bitcoin address, alleged FSB funding interest (reported as a conversation, not independently confirmed), software-company-like development, testing against endpoint detection, phishing templates, a control-panel screenshot, infrastructure discussions, and operators named Stern and Bentley discussing delivery/logistics; Stern discussed a desire to start a cryptocurrency, with no evidence it happened. Rapid7 assessed Conti remained active/significant after infrastructure takedown and observed normal operations resuming by March 9, 2022. SRC-003 (Center for Internet Security) describes Conti as a notorious RaaS operation using double extortion and apparent ties to TrickBot. It states leaks published after Conti's Russia-support statement exposed internal communications and, according to reporting it cites, Conti's handlers divided work into business units: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, and Penetration Testers/Hackers, with spamming contracted externally; each department had a budget, units could borrow funds, and an insider claimed more than 60 personnel in July 2021. It reports spending on Cobalt Strike, security/antivirus software, and Windows 11 reverse engineering. It says the larger platform later shut down/rebranded into smaller units, and later the leak/negotiation infrastructure shut down. It reports MS-ISAC had 22 Conti-related SLTT attacks from mid-2020-Jan 2022 and open-source reporting tied Conti to Ryuk, but these are reported/assessed claims. SRC-004 (DOJ Middle District of Tennessee) identifies case 3:23-CR-00092 defendants Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov and case 3:23-CR-00088 defendant Oleksii Lytvynenko. DOJ says Conti affected more than 900 victims globally; the four first defendants were charged in Sept. 2023 with conspiracy to violate CFAA and conspiracy to commit wire fraud, and Lytvynenko was charged in Nov. 2025 with conspiracy to deploy Conti; DOJ notes all defendants are presumed innocent. Lytvynenko later pleaded guilty to Count 2 conspiracy to commit wire fraud on June 10, 2026, with sentencing scheduled Sept. 10, 2026."}
